XXVI. JUDICIAL INDEPENDENCE

The courts shall remain independent.

The Krönherr is the supreme executive authority, but judges shall independently determine criminal guilt and apply the law.

Austria shall maintain a Constitutional Court.

The Constitutional Court shall determine whether governmental actions comply with the Constitution.

The Krönherr shall not be able to simply dismiss judges because they disagree with him.

Judges may be removed for:

corruption

serious misconduct

criminal conduct

permanent incapacity

other constitutionally defined reasons


Citizens shall possess access to legal representation and appeals.


---

XXVII. CRIMINAL JUSTICE

No person shall be considered guilty merely because someone accuses them.

Criminal punishment shall require lawful procedures and sufficient evidence.

The state shall protect:

due process

legal representation

judicial review

appeals

lawful evidence standards

protection against arbitrary punishment


Serious crimes shall receive severe penalties.

Murder of one person shall ordinarily carry life imprisonment.

Murder of multiple people may carry the death penalty under the constitutional criminal code.

The death penalty shall require exceptionally high evidentiary standards and multiple judicial reviews.

The law shall distinguish:

intentional murder

accidental death

lawful self-defence

negligent killing

attempted murder

other forms of homicide


No death penalty shall be imposed merely because someone was suspected of murder.


---

XXVIII. PRISONERS AND CHILDREN

A person sentenced to prison serves the sentence personally.

A child shall never be treated as a prisoner because of the parent's imprisonment.

If an imprisoned person gives birth, the child shall be placed with suitable alternative caregivers or another appropriate arrangement.

The child shall receive protection, healthcare, education, and care.

Upon release, the parent may seek custody through the ordinary judicial system.

Custody shall ultimately depend upon the child's welfare and applicable family law.


